{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778946127,"nonce":"A4Adt7T+RcneXVNCskINHTSZAIo=","message":"## Design clarification: actors, principals, leases, and auth management\n\nThe ledger should model attribution and audit facts, but it should not become the secret store or the sole authority for credential validity.\n\nRecommended distinction:\n\n- `actor`: the durable identity that performed or initiated work. Examples: configured Mandible user, Codex agent, Claude agent, Gemini agent, Mandible system, git-bug adapter.\n- `principal`: the concrete credential identity used to act. Examples: SSH signing key fingerprint, YubiKey-backed key identity, API-key account identity, Claude Code login identity, generated agent key fingerprint.\n- `credential_lease`: a time-bounded or scope-bounded authorization for a principal/capability. Examples: this agent session may use this repo agent key in this worktree until expiry/revocation; this auth executor may perform one protected merge.\n- `ledger_event`: the immutable record of what happened. Examples: actor X requested auth; actor Y approved; actor Z executed merge using principal P under lease L; issue I was closed by auth request A.\n\nImplementation consequence:\n\n- Ledger rows should reference `actor_id`, optional `principal_id` / public fingerprint, optional `lease_id`, source, and provenance metadata.\n- Raw private keys, OAuth refresh tokens, API keys, and other secrets should not live in the ledger.\n- Auth/key management remains the authority for whether a credential or lease is valid. The ledger records issuance/revocation/use events and lets the UI explain who did what with which public credential identity.\n- Auth requests should record requester, approver, executor, principal/fingerprint, lease/capability when known, target issues, target branch, and resulting commits.\n- Agent-created comments/issues/commits should preserve both the canonical actor and the credential/principal used when available. Imported git-bug authors can initially enter as actor aliases or external actors until reconciled.\n\nPossible schema additions, either in ledger core or this integration issue depending on scope:\n\n```sql\nactors (\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  kind TEXT NOT NULL,\n  display_name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  provider TEXT,\n  metadata_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  updated_at INTEGER NOT NULL\n);\n\nactor_aliases (\n  actor_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  alias_kind TEXT NOT NULL,\n  alias_value TEXT NOT NULL,\n  source TEXT NOT NULL,\n  created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (alias_kind, alias_value)\n);\n\nprincipals (\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  actor_id TEXT,\n  kind TEXT NOT NULL,\n  public_identifier TEXT NOT NULL,\n  provider TEXT,\n  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active',\n  created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  revoked_at INTEGER,\n  metadata_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'\n);\n\ncredential_leases (\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  principal_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  actor_id TEXT,\n  scope_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n  scope_id TEXT,\n  capability TEXT NOT NULL,\n  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active',\n  issued_at INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  expires_at INTEGER,\n  revoked_at INTEGER,\n  metadata_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'\n);\n```\n\nThese tables do not need to make Mandible a general-purpose IAM system. They are enough to keep the ledger, auth queue, agent key management, and future session/conversation UI from disagreeing about identity and provenance.\n\nDone condition for this issue should include at least: auth request issue links and ledger events preserve requester/approver/executor actor IDs, public credential identity when known, and lease/capability IDs when the auth subsystem provides them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}